{%- comment -%}
  price — THE money-formatting rule for the whole theme. One line of output,
  no markup, no wrapper:

    {%- render 'price', amount: product.price -%}          → $240   or $240.00
    {%- render 'price', amount: v.compare_at_price -%}

  WHY THIS EXISTS: the audit found the same product printed as "$240.00" in one
  place and "$240" in another on the same store. That is not a style
  disagreement, it is the store looking like two different stores. A shopper
  reads it as sloppiness about money, on the surface where sloppiness about
  money costs the most.

  The rule is now a setting, `settings.price_show_cents`:
    off (default) → `money_without_trailing_zeros` — $240, but $240.50 keeps its
                    cents, because dropping them would state the wrong price
    on            → `money` — always two decimals, which is what a shop selling
                    at .99 or in a currency with meaningful minor units needs

  Falls back to OFF if the setting is absent, which is exactly the behaviour
  every price in this theme had before the setting existed — so a store that
  updates the theme files without updating settings_data.json sees no change.

  `amount` is CENTS (Shopify's own unit), never a formatted string. Passing a
  string silently prints it back unformatted, so pass `product.price`, never
  `product.price | money`.

  Renders nothing at all for a nil amount: `compare_at_price` is nil on most
  products and `nil | money` prints "$0.00", which on a strike-through line
  would advertise a fictional original price of zero.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign price_show_cents = settings.price_show_cents | default: false
  assign price_out = ''
  unless amount == nil
    if price_show_cents
      assign price_out = amount | money
    else
      assign price_out = amount | money_without_trailing_zeros
    endif
  endunless
-%}
{{- price_out -}}
